om/item?id=9679243","site":"Hacker News","score":102,"comments":127,"year":2015},{"title":"The Great Ivy League Nude Posture Photo Scandal (1995)","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=6720621","site":"Hacker News","score":144,"comments":71,"year":2013},{"title":"Posture for a Healthy Back","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=10115955","site":"Hacker News","score":104,"comments":94,"year":2015},{"title":"Ask HN: How do you keep a good posture when you spend most of your type sitting?","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=16529054","site":"Hacker News","score":115,"comments":82,"year":2018},{"title":"Ask HN: Tricks to improve posture?","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=22892205","site":"Hacker News","score":89,"comments":57,"year":2020},{"title":"Posture: Is the Pandemic Breaking Our Backs?","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=26554261","site":"Hacker News","score":70,"comments":67,"year":2021},{"title":"Slouch: Posture panic in modern America","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=42211561","site":"Hacker News","score":71,"comments":60,"year":2024},{"title":"How Posture Makes Us Human","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=17053702","site":"Hacker News","score":82,"comments":41,"year":2018},{"title":"Posture Affects Standing, and Not Just the Physical Kind","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=10806658","site":"Hacker News","score":89,"comments":31,"year":2015}],"web":[{"title":"Power pose","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_pose"},{"title":"Posture (psychology)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Posture_(psychology)"},{"title":"Power posing (Grokipedia)","url":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Power_posing"}]},"angle":{"goal":"Posture","chain":["Stand upright with open posture","Taller, broader visual impression","Look and feel more confident"],"effect":"direct","text":"Users hold an upright, open stance, sometimes as power posing, so they look taller, broader and more confident while standing. The visual change is immediate; hormone and behaviour claims from posing have replicated poorly, while effects on how people feel are better supported."},"wikipedia":null,"grokipedia":null,"lead":["In looksmaxxing and self-improvement communities, \"posture\", \"frame\" and \"body language\" refer to how a person holds and moves the body, with the belief that upright, open and expansive postures make a person look and feel more confident and attractive. \"Power posing\" is a closely related and widely publicised version.","Research on posture reports that confident postures can influence self-evaluation and mood, but the hormonal and behavioral claims of power posing have not replicated well.[1][2] Reviews find a reliable effect on feelings of power but not on physiology.[1]"],"infobox":[["Type","Body-language practice (meta)"],["Related","Power posing, posture correction"],["Key study","Carney, Cuddy and Yap, 2010"],["Latest large review","Körner et al., 2022"]],"sections":[{"h":"Research on posture","paras":["Briñol, Petty and Wagner (2009) found that people writing self-descriptions in a confident posture rated themselves more favorably than those in a doubtful posture, and Riskind and Gotay (1982) reported more stress in hunched postures.[2] Open posture was rated as more capable in counseling settings (Ridley and Asbury, 1988).[2] Peper et al. (2018) reported better math scores with erect posture.[2]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"Power posing","paras":["Carney, Cuddy and Yap (2010) reported that expansive poses raised testosterone and lowered cortisol in 42 participants. Ranehill et al. (2015) replicated only the felt-power effect, and in 2016 Carney said she no longer believed the effects were real.[1] A 2022 review of 128 studies by Körner et al. found reliable effects on thoughts and feelings, none on physiology and unclear effects on behavior; Cuddy and colleagues' 2018 meta-analysis reported evidential value for postural feedback.[1]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"In looksmaxxing culture","paras":["Forum members discuss \"frame\" as an overall presence combining posture, movement and confidence. These are community beliefs; no published trial tests them as appearance methods."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Clinical studies","paras":["Studies of posture and mood exist, and power-posing trials are summarized above, but no studies were found on \"frame\" as defined by looksmaxxing communities.[1][2]"],"subs":[]}],"lore":null,"timeline":[{"year":"2010","event":"Carney, Cuddy and Yap publish the power pose study.[1]"},{"year":"2012","event":"Cuddy TED talk.[1]"},{"year":"2015","event":"Ranehill et al. replication.[1]"},{"year":"2016","event":"Carney statement on the effects.[1]"},{"year":"2022","event":"Körner et al. review of 128 studies.[1]"}],"refs":[{"title":"Power pose","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_pose","source":"Wikipedia","year":null,"type":"encyclopedia"},{"title":"Posture (psychology)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Posture_(psychology)","source":"Wikipedia","year":null,"type":"encyclopedia"},{"title":"Power posing (Grokipedia)","url":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Power_posing","source":"Grokipedia","year":null,"type":"encyclopedia"}],"studies":[{"title":"Power pose","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_pose","source":"Wikipedia","year":null,"kind":null},{"title":"Posture (psychology)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Posture_(psychology)","source":"Wikipedia","year":null,"kind":null}],"reddit":[],"forums":[{"title":"Posture Maxxing","url":"https://looksmax.org/threads/posture-maxxing.1778667/","site":"looksmax.org","archived":"https://web.archive.org/web/20251230035840/https://looksmax.org/threads/posture-maxxing.1778667/"},{"title":"posture","url":"https://looksmax.org/threads/posture.1768450/","site":"looksmax.org","archived":"https://web.archive.org/web/20251218125846/https://looksmax.org/threads/posture.1768450/"},{"title":"Posture is truly one of the most important things and the reason is simple.","url":"https://looks